  "question": {
    "text": "A heat source at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> is connected to another heat reservoir at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> by a copper slab which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> thick. Given that the thermal conductivity of copper is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.1</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, the energy flux through it in the steady-state is:",
