  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given,</p><p>Temperature of heat source,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>,</p><p>Temperature of heat reservoir,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>,</p><p>Conductivity of copper&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>From the equation of steady-state heat flow,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Heat energy flux&nbsp;can be defined as the rate of heat energy transfer through a given surface.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></p></blockquote>",
